(a) Upon the receipt of a written request, without regard to form, signed by:
(1) Any public authority;
(2) Five citizens of the city;
(3) Any building inspector of the city;
(4) Any fire marshal of the city;
(5) Any police officer of the city; or
(6) Any other elected or appointed official of the city, having charge of any activities concerning dwellings, buildings or structures.
(b) Based upon personal observation or reasonable belief that any dwelling, building, or structure is:
(1) Unfit for human habitation;
(2) Is unfit for commercial, industrial, or business use;
(3) Is vacant, dilapidated, and is being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes; or
(4) Has an accumulation of weeds, trash, junk, filth, and other unsanitary or unsafe conditions which create a public health hazard or a general nuisance to those persons residing in the vicinity.
(c) The buildings hearing officer shall issue and cause to be served upon the owner of, and all parties in interest, in such dwelling, building or structure a complaint stating the charges in that respect and containing a notice:
(1) That a hearing will be held before the buildings hearing officer or his/her designated agent, in the city, not less than ten nor more than 30 days after receipt by the owner and all parties in interest in the property, of the complaint;
(2) That the owner of, and all parties in interest, in such dwelling, building or structure has the right to file a written answer;
(3) Appear in person or otherwise, and give testimony; and
(4) That the rules of evidence prevailing in courts of law or equity shall not be controlling in the hearing before the buildings hearing officer.
(Code 1977, § 11-51)

552:
After the notice and hearing provided for in section 18-137, the buildings hearing officer may determine whether a dwelling, building or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial, or business use, or that other unsanitary or unsafe conditions exist which create a public health hazard or a general nuisance, by finding that the following conditions exist in or around such dwelling, building or structure:
(1) Those dangerous or injurious to the health, safety, or morals of the occupants of such dwelling, building or structure;
(2) Those dangerous or injurious to the health, safety, or morals of the occupants of neighborhood dwellings, buildings or structures;
(3) Those dangerous or injurious to the health, safety, or morals of other residents of the city or county;
(4) A dwelling, building or structure is vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with drug crimes, upon personal observation or report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of drug crimes being committed;
(5) An accumulation of weeds, trash, junk, filth, and other unsanitary or unsafe conditions which create a public health hazard or a general nuisance; or
(6) Any of the conditions, without limiting the generality of subsections (1)—(5) of this section, listed in O.C.G.A. § 41-2-10, which is made a part hereof by reference.
(Code 1977, § 11-52)

555:
At any regular or called meeting of mayor and council, after receiving the report provided in section 18-139, or upon the appeal to mayor and council of any action as provided in section 18-140, the mayor and council as the governing body of the city may:
(1) Dismiss the complaint;
(2) Uphold and ratify the findings of the buildings hearing officer;
(3) By ordinance, order the buildings hearing officer to proceed to effectuate the purpose of O.C.G.A. §§ 41-2-7—41-2-17 with respect to the particular property the buildings hearing officer shall have found to be unfit for human habitation or unfit for its current commercial, industrial, or business use, which property shall be described in a manner sufficient to identify the property;
(4) Determine the value of the dwelling, building, or structure and determine the cost of repair, alteration or improvement thereof;
(5) Assess the cost of the proceeding against the proper party;
(6) Determine that a dwelling, building or structure is vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with drug crimes, upon personal observation or report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of drug crimes being committed;
(7) Determine that any property has an accumulation of weeds, trash, junk, filth, and other unsanitary or unsafe conditions which create a public health hazard or a general nuisance;
(8) Do any or all of the procedures listed in subsections (1) through (7) of this section as justice may require and any thing or act which will protect the lives and property and health, safety, and welfare or otherwise prevent conditions inimical to the welfare of the citizens of the city, county, or state.
(Code 1977, § 11-55)

572:
The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this article, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Administrative fee means the component of an occupation tax which approximates the reasonable cost of handling and processing the registration of the business from whom the occupation tax is collected.
Dominant line means the type of business within a multiple service or product business from which the greatest amount of revenue is derived.
Gross receipts shall have the meaning as defined under O.C.G.A. § 48-13-5(2)(A), which section, as amended from time to time, is hereby incorporated by reference.
Location or office means a fixed place located within the city from which a person conducts business, but shall not include a work site located in the city for less than four months in any calendar year for the purpose of serving a single customer or project.
Occupation tax means a tax levied on persons, partnerships, corporations or other entities for engaging in an occupation, profession or business for revenue purposes.
Occupation tax certificate means a document issued by the city acknowledging payment of the occupation tax and administrative fee.
Person includes sole proprietors, practitioners of professions, corporations, partnerships or any other form of organization.
Practitioners of professions are:
(1) Lawyers;
(2) Physicians licensed under Chapter 34 of Title 43;
(3) Osteopaths licensed under Chapter 34 of Title 43;
(4) Chiropractors;
(5) Podiatrists;
(6) Dentists;
(7) Optometrists;
(8) Psychologists;
(9) Veterinarians;
(10) Landscape architects;
(11) Land surveyors;
(12) Practitioners of physiotherapy;
(13) Public accountants;
(14) Embalmers;
(15) Funeral directors;
(16) Civil, mechanical, hydraulic or electrical engineers;
(17) Architects; and
(18) Marriage and family therapists, social workers and professional counselors.
Such term shall not include a practitioner who is an employee of a business, if the business pays an occupation tax.
Profitability class means the category into which an occupation is placed for occupation tax purposes in accordance with section 22-36 [this article] of this Code.
Regulatory fee means the payment required by the city as an exercise of its police power as part of or as an aid to regulation of an occupation, profession or business, the amount of which shall approximate the reasonable cost of the actual regulatory activity performed by the city.
Regulatory fee certificate means a document issued by the city acknowledging payment of regulatory fee.
Standard industrial classification means categories of occupations and industries established by and promulgated by the Office of Management and Budget of the United States of America and found in the Standard Industrial Classification Manual of 1987.
(Ord. No. 94-18, § 1, 12-5-94; Ord. No. 2020-21, 12-7-20)
Editor's note— Ordinance No. 94-18, adopted Dec. 5, 1994, repealed §§ 22-26—22-29. Formerly, §§ 22-26—22-29 pertained to license required; license period, proration; late payment penalty; and display of license, respectively, and derived from §§ 14-1—14-4 of the 1977 Code. Ordinance No. 94-18 provided for the addition of new provisions which have been included herein as §§ 22-26—22-29.2 at the editor's discretion.

